course: biology
lesson: 70
title: Senses: nose, tongue and skin
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You smell cookies baking from the other room because tiny invisible bits of the cookies have floated through the air, up your nose, and landed on smell cells that tell your brain «cookies!».
explain: Smell and taste work as a team: when a cold blocks your nose, your dinner tastes of almost nothing, because much of what we call flavour is really smell.
explain: Your tongue's bumps hold taste buds for sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami, and every part of the tongue can taste all five; the old «tongue map» is a myth.
explain: Your skin is your biggest sense organ, with sensors for touch, pressure, heat, cold and pain, and some tricks fool them: a chilli feels hot though it is not, and mint feels cold though it is not.
explain: Animals push these senses much further: a sniffer dog can smell things hidden in a suitcase, and a cat feels its way through gaps with its whiskers.
fact: Smells are tiny bits of a substance floating in the air; they land on smell cells high inside the nose, which send signals to the brain.
fact: Much of flavour is smell: when the nose is blocked, food smells cannot reach the smell cells at the back of the nose, so food seems to taste of nothing.
fact: A dog has around 200 to 300 million smell cells against our roughly 5 to 6 million, and a big part of its brain works on smells, so sniffer dogs can find hidden things in bags.
fact: Taste buds sense five basic tastes: sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami, the savoury taste of cheese and tomatoes; many poisons taste bitter, so bitterness can warn us.
fact: The «tongue map», with sweet only at the tip and bitter only at the back, is a myth; all parts of the tongue with taste buds can taste all five tastes.
fact: The bumps on the tongue are papillae, and many of them hold taste buds; an adult has about 2,000 to 10,000 taste buds, and they are replaced about every week or two.
fact: Chillies contain capsaicin, which switches on the nerve sensors that detect heat, so the mouth feels burning though nothing is hot; birds do not feel it, which helps them spread chilli seeds; milk washes the burning away better than water.
fact: Menthol in mint switches on the nerve sensors that detect cold, so the mouth feels cool though it is not colder.
fact: The skin has several kinds of touch sensors: some feel light touch, some pressure, some vibration and some stretching, which is how we tell silk from sandpaper.
fact: Fingertips are packed with touch sensors and can feel two points just 2 to 3 millimetres apart, while on the back two points a few centimetres apart can feel like one; that is why blind people read the tiny raised dots of Braille with their fingertips.
fact: Skin judges hot and cold by change: after warm water, lukewarm water feels cool, and after cold water, the same lukewarm water feels warm.
fact: Pain sensors send fast signals to the spinal cord, which makes the hand jerk away from a hot radiator even before the brain feels the pain.
fact: Cat whiskers are thick, deep-rooted hairs with many nerve endings that sense touch and tiny movements of air, and they help a cat judge whether it can fit through a gap.
exercise: How do you smell cookies baking in another room? | Tiny bits of the cookies float through the air into your nose | The sound of the oven travels through the house and tells your nose | Your eyes see the smell through the wall | The heat of the oven reaches your tongue
exercise: Why does food taste of almost nothing when you have a cold? | The blocked nose stops food smells reaching the smell cells | The cold germs kill every one of your taste buds for a week or more | The food really has less taste in winter | Your teeth stop working properly
exercise: About how many smell cells does a dog have? | Around 200 to 300 million | Around 5 to 6 million, like us | Around 100, far fewer than us | Around 2 billion billion of them
exercise: Why can a sniffer dog find things hidden in a bag? | It has far more smell cells and a big smell part of its brain | It can see straight through the bag with its special night-time eyes | It hears the things inside the bag move about | It guesses from the colour of the bag
exercise: What are the five basic tastes? | Sweet, salty, sour, bitter and umami | Sweet, salty, freezing, boiling and spicy | Sweet, sour, crunchy, chewy and soggy | Salty, minty, spicy, sweet and fruity
exercise: Which taste is the savoury taste of cheese and tomatoes? | Umami | Sour | Bitter | Sweet
exercise: Which part of the tongue can taste sweet? | All parts with taste buds | Only the very tip of the tongue | Only the back of the tongue | Only the two sides of the tongue
exercise: What are the little bumps on your tongue? | Papillae, many of which hold taste buds | Tiny teeth that help you chew your food | Spots from eating too many sweets | Holes where the saliva comes out
exercise: About how many taste buds does an adult have? | About 2,000 to 10,000 | About 20 | About a million | About 5
exercise: Why does a chilli make your mouth burn? | Its capsaicin switches on the nerve sensors for heat | It really is much hotter inside than a pan of boiling water | It has tiny sharp spikes | It makes your teeth catch fire
exercise: Why does mint toothpaste feel cold? | Its menthol switches on the nerve sensors for cold | It is kept in a big fridge at the shop until you buy it | It has ice inside the tube | It freezes your saliva
exercise: How can you tell silk from sandpaper with your eyes shut? | Different touch sensors in the skin feel texture and pressure | Your skin can see the colours of the things that it touches | Sandpaper makes a scratchy sound that you hear | Silk is always much warmer than sandpaper
exercise: Where can you feel two pencil points that are very close together as two? | On a fingertip | On the middle of the back | On the tip of the elbow | On the back of the leg
exercise: After one hand is in warm water and one in cold, both go into lukewarm water. How does it feel? | Cool to the warm hand and warm to the cold hand | Warm to both hands, because lukewarm water is warm | Cold to both hands, because it is cooler than skin | Exactly the same to both hands, just nicely lukewarm
exercise: Why does your hand jerk away from a hot radiator before you feel the pain? | Pain signals go to the spinal cord, which moves the hand first | Your hand decides on its own, because it has a small brain of its own | The heat pushes your hand away | Your eyes saw the heat coming
exercise: What do a cat's whiskers do? | Sense touch and air movements to help judge gaps | Help the cat smell mice hiding behind the cupboards | Keep its face warm in winter | Help it hear better
test: my cousin says the tip of the tongue does sweet and the back does bitter. right? | No, that tongue map is a myth: all parts taste all five | Yes, each taste has its own area and they never mix at all | Yes, but only for children | No, only the middle can taste anything
test: In a cartoon a dragon eats a chilli and real flames shoot out of his mouth. What is really happening when we eat chilli? | Capsaicin tricks heat sensors, so it feels hot but nothing burns | Chillies really heat the mouth up to the temperature of boiling water | Chillies make the stomach catch fire | Chillies are full of tiny sparks
test: What would happen if you held your nose while eating a strawberry sweet? | It would taste far less of strawberry, mostly just sweet | It would taste twice as strong | It would taste salty | It would taste of nothing at all, not even a little bit sweet
test: argh chilli!! water or milk, quick? | Milk helps more than water to wash the burning away | Water, loads of it, and the burning stops at once | Neither, eat another chilli | Blow on your tongue and it stops
test: Why can a bird eat a chilli pepper that would make a person cry? | Birds do not feel capsaicin | Birds have no tongues at all | Birds swallow it so fast it cannot burn | Birds have fireproof stomachs
test: Ali's friend presses two pencils on his back, close together, and he feels one. Why? | The back has fewer touch sensors, spread far apart | His friend is pressing much too softly to be felt | Two pencils feel just the same as one | His back has gone to sleep
test: Why does a blind person read Braille with their fingertips and not their elbow? | Fingertips are packed with touch sensors and feel tiny dots apart | Elbows are too far away from the eyes and the brain to read with them | Fingertips can see the dots | Elbows have no skin on them
test: My dad cut our cat's whiskers so she looks neat. Is that ok? | No, she needs whiskers to feel her way and judge gaps | Yes, whiskers are only there to make a cat look pretty | Yes, they help her smell better short | No, it makes all her fur fall out
test: In a game your hero tastes poison and the bitter warning shows on screen. Why is a bitter taste useful in real life? | Many poisons are bitter, so bitterness can warn us | Bitter things are always healthy, so we should eat more of them | Bitter taste makes food go down faster | Bitter means the food is sweet inside
test: piss off with the mint gum, my mouth's freezing. is it actually colder?? | No, menthol just switches on your cold sensors | Yes, mint drops your mouth to below freezing | Yes, gum is made of ice | No, it is actually much hotter
test: A sniffer dog at the airport sits down beside a bag. Why trust its nose? | Dogs have hundreds of millions of smell cells, far more than us | Dogs can read the labels on bags | Dogs just guess and are often lucky | Dogs can see inside bags
exam: Identify the principal reason for which food appears tasteless when the nasal passages are obstructed. | Flavour depends largely upon smell, which cannot reach the smell cells | The taste buds cease to function whenever the nasal passages are obstructed | The saliva is no longer produced | The food loses its chemical content
exam: It is asserted that each region of the tongue perceives a single basic taste. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false; all regions bearing taste buds perceive all five | The assertion is true without exception in all persons of whatever age | The assertion is true for sweetness only | The assertion is true in adults only
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sensation produced by capsaicin? | It activates the sensors for heat without raising the temperature | It raises the temperature of the mouth to boiling point | It destroys the taste buds upon contact | It activates the sensors for cold
exam: Ascertain the reason for which lukewarm water feels cool to a hand previously immersed in warm water. | The skin perceives temperature relative to its preceding state | The water is itself cooled upon contact with the warmer hand | The hand loses all sensation in warm water | Warm water damages the sensors for cold
